WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.455
Welcome to Module 4 of The Zero
Trust Assessment as Evidence:

2
00:00:04.455 --> 00:00:07.275
reconcile, re-run and keep the evidence.

3
00:00:07.275 --> 00:00:09.100
A single run is a reading.

4
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.985
A program needs the readings over
time, lined up with the other tools,

5
00:00:12.985 --> 00:00:16.038
and sealed so an assessor
can check them months later.

6
00:00:16.038 --> 00:00:17.788
This module does all three.

7
00:00:17.788 --> 00:00:22.575
It covers Steps 9 and 10 of the Zero
Trust Assessment implementation guide,

8
00:00:22.575 --> 00:00:26.030
takes about fifty minutes,
and finishes the path.

9
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:30.930
Why does this matter?

10
00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.755
A single run is a reading.

11
00:00:32.755 --> 00:00:34.855
A program needs three more things.

12
00:00:34.855 --> 00:00:39.318
First, the readings over time: which
findings were fixed, which came back,

13
00:00:39.318 --> 00:00:43.630
and which only seemed to disappear
because the run lost a connection.

14
00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.818
Second, one finding per setting.

15
00:00:45.818 --> 00:00:50.591
The Zero Trust Assessment's findings
have to line up with ScubaGear's, and,

16
00:00:50.591 --> 00:00:53.534
where the agency runs
AWS, with Security Hub's,

17
00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:57.592
so one setting under one control
is one finding with one owner.

18
00:00:57.592 --> 00:01:01.180
And third, every run sealed, so
an assessor can check months

19
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.768
later that the files are the
ones the tool and the kit wrote.

20
00:01:04.768 --> 00:01:09.425
In this module you'll compare two
runs, reconcile through the control,

21
00:01:09.425 --> 00:01:14.290
set a cadence, and seal an evidence
package, then break it on purpose.

22
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.953
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

23
00:01:20.953 --> 00:01:24.644
You'll compare two runs, and
explain the kinds of change,

24
00:01:24.644 --> 00:01:26.777
including no longer evaluated.

25
00:01:26.777 --> 00:01:29.317
You'll say why runs from
different tool versions

26
00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:31.540
aren't compared without a recorded review.

27
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.317
You'll reconcile a finding across
the Zero Trust Assessment,

28
00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:40.378
ScubaGear and Security Hub, through the
control, and state what each tool can't see.

29
00:01:40.378 --> 00:01:44.006
And you'll set a cadence and
seal a run's evidence package,

30
00:01:44.006 --> 00:01:46.452
then check it, and see a broken one fail.

31
00:01:46.452 --> 00:01:50.890
You need Module 3's Lab 1, because
this lab uses its out folder.

32
00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:54.930
In Unit 2 you'll read the section
on reconciling at the control,

33
00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:59.500
in Evidence, Not Verdicts, a chapter
of Zero Trust and Assessment.

34
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.012
Unit 1 compares runs.

35
00:02:05.012 --> 00:02:09.571
The kit's comparison sorts
every test into new, regressed,

36
00:02:09.571 --> 00:02:14.324
fixed, still open, no longer
evaluated, changed or unchanged.

37
00:02:14.324 --> 00:02:16.700
New is a finding that wasn't there before.

38
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.012
Regressed is a pass that now fails.

39
00:02:19.012 --> 00:02:22.112
Fixed now passes, and
still open still fails.

40
00:02:22.112 --> 00:02:25.626
No longer evaluated is
the one that matters most.

41
00:02:25.626 --> 00:02:28.385
It's a finding that's
now skipped, planned,

42
00:02:28.385 --> 00:02:31.313
unable to evaluate, or
missing from the run.

43
00:02:31.313 --> 00:02:33.654
It isn't fixed, and counting it as fixed

44
00:02:33.654 --> 00:02:36.600
would turn a lost connection
into remediation.

45
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.888
So fix the run, and run it again.

46
00:02:38.888 --> 00:02:43.423
And one more rule: a new tool release
adds tests and moves every denominator,

47
00:02:43.423 --> 00:02:47.007
so the kit refuses to compare
runs from different versions

48
00:02:47.007 --> 00:02:50.590
until someone records that
they reviewed the change.

49
00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.915
Unit 2 reconciles.

50
00:02:55.915 --> 00:02:59.715
Tools with different identifiers can't
be matched to each other directly.

51
00:02:59.715 --> 00:03:03.539
Instead, each is matched to a
common spine: the setting itself,

52
00:03:03.539 --> 00:03:08.815
and the control it supports in NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three.

53
00:03:08.815 --> 00:03:12.692
When two tools fail the same
setting under the same control,

54
00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:16.965
that's one finding, with two pieces
of evidence, routed to one owner.

55
00:03:16.965 --> 00:03:21.584
CISA maps every SCuBA policy to
those controls, and where both fail,

56
00:03:21.584 --> 00:03:25.131
the ScubaGear finding is
the directive's deviation

57
00:03:25.131 --> 00:03:28.265
and carries B O D twenty-five
oh-one's reporting.

58
00:03:28.265 --> 00:03:33.113
On AWS, each Security Hub finding
lists its related requirements,

59
00:03:33.113 --> 00:03:35.627
such as A C four, and that's the spine.

60
00:03:35.627 --> 00:03:38.102
Two cautions from AWS itself.

61
00:03:38.102 --> 00:03:41.787
Its NIST standard supports only
a subset of the framework,

62
00:03:41.787 --> 00:03:43.552
with automated checks only.

63
00:03:43.552 --> 00:03:48.593
And a control with no corresponding resources
reports passed at the account level,

64
00:03:48.593 --> 00:03:51.003
so a pass can mean nothing was evaluated.

65
00:03:51.003 --> 00:03:53.770
Each tool sees only its own platform.

66
00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:58.832
Where are agencies?

67
00:03:58.832 --> 00:04:00.797
Most run more than one platform,

68
00:04:00.797 --> 00:04:03.895
and each has its own posture
tool on the same spine.

69
00:04:03.895 --> 00:04:08.496
For Microsoft three sixty-five G C C
and Azure, the workbook's baseline,

70
00:04:08.496 --> 00:04:10.514
it's the Zero Trust Assessment,

71
00:04:10.514 --> 00:04:15.195
with ScubaGear as the directive's
tool for Microsoft three sixty-five.

72
00:04:15.195 --> 00:04:16.861
On AWS, it's Security Hub,

73
00:04:16.861 --> 00:04:21.770
with its standard for NIST eight
hundred fifty-three, revision five.

74
00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:24.722
In AWS GovCloud, Security
Hub is available,

75
00:04:24.722 --> 00:04:30.458
with limits on product integrations, some
controls, and cross-Region aggregation.

76
00:04:30.458 --> 00:04:33.587
On Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,
it's Cloud Guard,

77
00:04:33.587 --> 00:04:36.482
and on Google Cloud,
Security Command Center.

78
00:04:36.482 --> 00:04:40.896
The chapter on cloud-native posture
in Security Operations and Telemetry

79
00:04:40.896 --> 00:04:45.096
compares those two, and how their
findings enter the evidence contract.

80
00:04:45.096 --> 00:04:49.697
The rule is the same everywhere: each
finding is matched to the setting

81
00:04:49.697 --> 00:04:54.580
and the control, with one owner, in
one row of the reconciliation table.

82
00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.642
Unit 3 is the cadence
and the evidence package.

83
00:05:01.642 --> 00:05:03.676
The cadence is design guidance,

84
00:05:03.676 --> 00:05:08.292
from the day-two runbook in Compliance
Automation and Day-2 Operations.

85
00:05:08.292 --> 00:05:10.906
Monthly, a full run, with the results and

86
00:05:10.906 --> 00:05:14.092
the plan of action and
milestones regenerated.

87
00:05:14.092 --> 00:05:18.183
After each remediation wave,
a run of the affected pillar,

88
00:05:18.183 --> 00:05:21.317
so a fix is closed by
evidence, not by a ticket.

89
00:05:21.317 --> 00:05:23.988
Before a tool upgrade reaches production,

90
00:05:23.988 --> 00:05:26.581
a run of the new version
in the test tenant.

91
00:05:26.581 --> 00:05:28.630
And quarterly, a trend review.

92
00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:33.283
Microsoft's operations guidance for
Global Secure Access includes a weekly

93
00:05:33.283 --> 00:05:38.155
scheduled digest; the guide explains two
cautions before adopting it as written.

94
00:05:38.155 --> 00:05:41.953
The evidence package for a run
holds the export and its hash,

95
00:05:41.953 --> 00:05:44.665
the normalized results, the dispositions,

96
00:05:44.665 --> 00:05:49.470
the reviews and exceptions with their
hashes, the register, the comparison,

97
00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:53.640
and the chained records with the
S H A two fifty-six sums file.

98
00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:58.115
Try it.

99
00:05:58.115 --> 00:06:01.040
Lab 1 picks up Module 3's two runs.

100
00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.457
Step one compares them: two new,
one regressed, three fixed,

101
00:06:05.457 --> 00:06:11.378
twelve still open, two no longer evaluated,
two changed, and twelve unchanged.

102
00:06:11.378 --> 00:06:13.527
Step two lists the ones to look at.

103
00:06:13.527 --> 00:06:17.403
Two one seven eight three
regressed, from pass to fail.

104
00:06:17.403 --> 00:06:21.590
Two one nine four one is new: it
wasn't in the first run at all.

105
00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:23.815
Now the two no longer evaluated.

106
00:06:23.815 --> 00:06:27.972
Two one eight six oh and four one
oh three two both failed last time,

107
00:06:27.972 --> 00:06:31.052
and this time they were
skipped as disconnected.

108
00:06:31.052 --> 00:06:34.490
The second run lost its Azure
and Exchange connections.

109
00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:38.128
Those findings aren't fixed; they
still need a run that connects.

110
00:06:38.128 --> 00:06:41.205
And four one oh five oh
shows as new only because the

111
00:06:41.205 --> 00:06:44.428
first run timed out on it,
not because something broke.

112
00:06:44.428 --> 00:06:48.710
The slide shortens step two's
command; the page has it in full.

113
00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.360
Step three seals the
second run's evidence.

114
00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:59.091
Each output folder becomes a
record: the normalized run,

115
00:06:59.091 --> 00:07:02.498
the register, and the
comparison, one after another.

116
00:07:02.498 --> 00:07:05.886
Each record names the
controls its folder supports.

117
00:07:05.886 --> 00:07:12.642
The normalized run supports A U nine, C A
two, C A seven and C M six; the register

118
00:07:12.642 --> 00:07:14.858
adds C A five and R A seven;

119
00:07:14.858 --> 00:07:19.510
the comparison supports A U
nine, C A seven and C M six.

120
00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:22.539
Each record also holds
every file's hash and the

121
00:07:22.539 --> 00:07:25.723
previous record's hash, so
the records form a chain.

122
00:07:25.723 --> 00:07:30.796
Step four checks the package: sixteen
files match the sums file, the chain of

123
00:07:30.796 --> 00:07:36.197
three records is unbroken, and a passing
record covers each step for the run date.

124
00:07:36.197 --> 00:07:39.336
The I S S O doesn't need
the kit to check this.

125
00:07:39.336 --> 00:07:41.980
In a POSIX shell, in the evidence folder,

126
00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:46.710
the standard sha two fifty-six sum
check reads the same sums file.

127
00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:52.947
Step five breaks the seal on purpose.

128
00:07:52.947 --> 00:07:54.811
Add one line, the word edited,

129
00:07:54.811 --> 00:07:59.023
to the register file inside the
sealed package, and check again.

130
00:07:59.023 --> 00:08:01.436
Two checks fail, and each says why.

131
00:08:01.436 --> 00:08:04.182
The sums check fails
because the register file

132
00:08:04.182 --> 00:08:06.648
no longer matches its
hash in the sums file.

133
00:08:06.648 --> 00:08:11.310
The chain check fails because the file no
longer matches the hash its record holds.

134
00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.785
That's the point of sealing.

135
00:08:12.785 --> 00:08:16.311
Months later, an assessor can
show that the files are the ones

136
00:08:16.311 --> 00:08:19.485
the tool and the kit wrote, or
see exactly which one isn't.

137
00:08:19.485 --> 00:08:22.310
Step six cleans up: delete the out folder.

138
00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:26.613
In a real program, the sealed folder
lives in the evidence repository,

139
00:08:26.613 --> 00:08:30.360
and retention follows the
records officer's schedule.

140
00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:37.825
Lab 2 makes one real change
and proves it with evidence,

141
00:08:37.825 --> 00:08:40.385
in your test tenant, never production.

142
00:08:40.385 --> 00:08:41.947
The authors haven't run it.

143
00:08:41.947 --> 00:08:47.090
Step one: make one change that a finding
in Module 3's Lab 2 register points to,

144
00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:51.735
through the page its remediation
field names, under a change record.

145
00:08:51.735 --> 00:08:55.101
Step two: re-run the
assessment into a new folder,

146
00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:58.035
then normalize it and write its register.

147
00:08:58.035 --> 00:09:00.705
Step three: compare it with the first run,

148
00:09:00.705 --> 00:09:04.185
seal all three output folders,
and check the package.

149
00:09:04.185 --> 00:09:06.710
The finding you fixed
should show as fixed.

150
00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:08.887
If it shows as no longer evaluated,

151
00:09:08.887 --> 00:09:12.697
the second run didn't connect
to the service that test reads.

152
00:09:12.697 --> 00:09:17.910
Step four is optional: where you have an
AWS test account with Security Hub and its

153
00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:21.022
NIST standard enabled,
pull its active findings,

154
00:09:21.022 --> 00:09:23.823
and add one row to the
reconciliation table.

155
00:09:23.823 --> 00:09:25.335
Step five backs it out.

156
00:09:25.335 --> 00:09:29.660
The slide shortens the lab;
the page lists every command.

157
00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:35.933
The federal requirement, in NIST special

158
00:09:35.933 --> 00:09:39.272
publication eight hundred
fifty-three, revision five.

159
00:09:39.272 --> 00:09:43.828
C A seven, continuous monitoring:
assessment at a defined frequency,

160
00:09:43.828 --> 00:09:49.034
with the changes between runs analyzed,
and results correlated across tools.

161
00:09:49.034 --> 00:09:52.997
The evidence is each comparison
and the reconciliation table.

162
00:09:52.997 --> 00:09:57.447
A U nine and A U eleven: the
evidence protected and kept.

163
00:09:57.447 --> 00:10:00.922
That's the records, the
sums file and the exports.

164
00:10:00.922 --> 00:10:04.059
And C A five: the plan
of action and milestones

165
00:10:04.059 --> 00:10:07.197
regenerated from each
run, from the register.

166
00:10:07.197 --> 00:10:10.385
No key security indicator
is listed for that one.

167
00:10:10.385 --> 00:10:14.185
The evidence supports the
controls; the assessor decides.

168
00:10:14.185 --> 00:10:18.704
Retention follows the records officer's
schedule; the program's default

169
00:10:18.704 --> 00:10:23.360
for assessment evidence, in Zero
Trust and Assessment, is three years.

170
00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.085
Check yourself with two questions.

171
00:10:29.085 --> 00:10:34.060
First: a finding failed last month, and
this month it's skipped, disconnected.

172
00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:34.860
What is it?

173
00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.873
The answer is b: no longer evaluated.

174
00:10:37.873 --> 00:10:39.110
It's not fixed.

175
00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:41.597
Fix the run's connection,
and run it again.

176
00:10:41.597 --> 00:10:45.884
Second: a Security Hub control
reports passed at the account level.

177
00:10:45.884 --> 00:10:47.022
What should you check?

178
00:10:47.022 --> 00:10:51.417
The answer is b: whether any
resource corresponds to the control,

179
00:10:51.417 --> 00:10:55.397
because AWS produces an
account-level pass when none does.

180
00:10:55.397 --> 00:10:57.897
Read the pass with the resource inventory.

181
00:10:57.897 --> 00:11:03.170
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

182
00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:07.745
To sum up.

183
00:11:07.745 --> 00:11:11.457
You compared two runs, and told
a fix from a lost connection.

184
00:11:11.457 --> 00:11:15.492
You reconciled across tools through
the setting and the control,

185
00:11:15.492 --> 00:11:17.583
on every platform the agency runs.

186
00:11:17.583 --> 00:11:18.858
You set a cadence.

187
00:11:18.858 --> 00:11:22.296
And you sealed, checked and
broke an evidence package.

188
00:11:22.296 --> 00:11:26.420
The evidence an I S S O keeps from
this module: each comparison,

189
00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:30.215
the reconciliation table,
and the chained records with

190
00:11:30.215 --> 00:11:33.845
the sums file and the exports,
by the records schedule.

191
00:11:33.845 --> 00:11:38.603
That finishes the learning path, The
Zero Trust Assessment as Evidence:

192
00:11:38.603 --> 00:11:42.232
four modules, about three
hours with each module's Lab 1.

193
00:11:42.232 --> 00:11:45.202
Lab 2, in your own test
tenant, isn't counted,

194
00:11:45.202 --> 00:11:49.394
because a full run on a large
tenant can take more than a day.

195
00:11:49.394 --> 00:11:51.261
For the ten steps in one place,

196
00:11:51.261 --> 00:11:55.070
go back to the Zero Trust
Assessment implementation guide.

197
00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:55.900
Thank you.
